---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import DownloadSizes from '../components/DownloadSizes.astro';
import InstallMarquee from '../components/InstallMarquee.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import { discoveryMethodTotal, discoveryServiceCount } from '../data/discovery-coverage';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const releaseUrl = 'https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli/releases';
const sourceExample = `git clone https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli.git
cd grr-cli
cargo build --release --locked
./target/release/grr`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install grr"
  description="Install grr on macOS, Linux, and Windows (x64 and ARM) with Homebrew, winget, or crates.io, or build from source. Release binaries are zero-config."
>
  <PageHero eyebrow="install grr" title="Pick the shortest path to your shell.">
    Prebuilt binaries are the easy default. If you want a package manager, a crates.io install, or a local build, the project documents each route below.
    <Fragment slot="aside">One generated command tree · {discoveryMethodTotal} methods across {discoveryServiceCount} APIs</Fragment>
  </PageHero>

  <InstallMarquee />

  <section class="install-section section" aria-labelledby="channels-title">
    <div class="container">
      <SectionIntro id="channels-title" eyebrow="release channels" title="Install the way you already ship software." copy="The published binary is named grr, even though the crates.io package is grr-cli because the short crate name grr is already taken." />
      <div class="install-grid mt-7 grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <article class="install-card card card-border border-base-300 bg-base-100 lg:col-span-2">
          <div class="card-body gap-1 p-5">
            <h2 class="card-title m-0 text-base">Prebuilt binaries</h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-base-content/70">Download the archive for your platform from GitHub Releases. The release workflow publishes Windows x64, Windows on ARM, Linux x64, and macOS ARM binaries with checksums. Every build is UPX-packed, so the archives stay small.</p>
            <DownloadSizes note="Compressed release archives for the current GitHub release. A dash means that platform is not published yet." />
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-sm mt-3 w-fit" href={releaseUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub Releases <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card card card-border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <div class="card-body gap-1 p-5">
            <h2 class="card-title m-0 text-base">macOS + Linux</h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-base-content/70">The repository&rsquo;s Homebrew tap serves arm64 macOS and x86_64 Linux builds. Tap it, trust it, then install by formula name.</p>
            <p class="install-note m-0 mt-2 text-xs text-(--quiet)">Since Homebrew 4.4, third-party taps are untrusted by default, so <code>brew trust</code> is required before Homebrew will install a formula from the tap.</p>
            <CodeBlock code={'brew tap debanjanbasu/homebrew\nbrew trust debanjanbasu/homebrew\nbrew install grr'} label="homebrew" />
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card card card-border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <div class="card-body gap-1 p-5">
            <h2 class="card-title m-0 text-base">Windows</h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-base-content/70">The Windows package is available through winget, for both x64 and ARM64 machines. The published manifest is 0.4.0, matching the current GitHub release; 0.5.0 is in preparation and will land in the repository when its manifest review clears.</p>
            <CodeBlock code="winget install debanjanbasu.grr" label="winget" />
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card card card-border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <div class="card-body gap-1 p-5">
            <h2 class="card-title m-0 text-base">crates.io</h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-base-content/70">The crate publishes as <code>grr-cli</code>; the installed executable is <code>grr</code>. The default CLI enables HTTP/3, so a crates.io source install needs nightly Rust and the <code>reqwest_unstable</code> cfg.</p>
            <CodeBlock code={'RUSTFLAGS="--cfg reqwest_unstable" cargo install grr-cli --locked'} label="cargo · unix" />
            <p class="install-note m-0 mt-2 text-xs text-(--quiet)">PowerShell: set <code>$env:RUSTFLAGS='--cfg reqwest_unstable'</code> before running cargo. Library users can choose stable Rust with <code>default-features = false</code> and select only the service features they need.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card card card-border border-base-300 bg-base-100">
          <div class="card-body gap-1 p-5">
            <h2 class="card-title m-0 text-base">OAuth setup</h2>
            <p class="m-0 text-[13px] text-base-content/70">Release binaries are zero-config: the OAuth client is compiled in at build time, so <code>grr auth login</code> works with no setup. Building from source? Run <code>grr auth setup</code> to create and store your own client, then log in.</p>
            <CodeBlock code={`grr auth login\ngrr gmail profile`} label="first run" />
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="source-section section" aria-labelledby="source-title">
    <div class="container source-grid grid grid-cols-1 items-center gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="source-copy reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">from source</span>
        <h2 id="source-title" class="mt-1.5 mb-2 text-[clamp(22px,2.6vw,32px)] leading-[1.15]">Build the exact revision you want to run.</h2>
        <p class="m-0 text-sm text-base-content/70">The repository pins Rust nightly in <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>. A local clone uses the repository&rsquo;s Cargo config, which sets the unstable reqwest cfg and the extra build-std settings needed by the CLI.</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-3 text-sm text-base-content/70">The default CLI enables HTTP/3 through reqwest&rsquo;s rustls/quinn path. The <code>grr_cli</code> library can use stable Rust with <code>default-features = false</code> and HTTP/2, while selecting services such as <code>gmail</code> as needed.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-outline mt-4" href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli#development" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read development notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock code={sourceExample} label="local clone" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split-section section" aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">next step</span>
      <h2 id="next-title" class="mt-1.5 mb-2 text-[clamp(22px,2.6vw,32px)] leading-[1.15]">Bring your own client, only if you build.</h2>
      <p>Release binaries need none of this — just run <code>grr auth login</code>. If you build from source, <code>grr auth setup</code> creates a Google Cloud project, enables the APIs, makes a Desktop OAuth client, and stores the client ID for you.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli/blob/main/docs/gcp-setup.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google Cloud setup guide ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli#60-second-quickstart" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">60-second quickstart ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href={base}>Back to the overview →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
